WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.618
Time to send a first real request, see why the reply can forget everything, and get output your code can trust.

00:00:07.818 --> 00:00:10.407
A user writes: hi, I am Chandra.

00:00:10.657 --> 00:00:12.224
The bot replies politely.

00:00:12.474 --> 00:00:15.806
One message later the user asks: what is my name?

00:00:16.056 --> 00:00:22.233
And the bot has no idea. The model did not forget. It never remembered, and this video shows why.

00:00:22.483 --> 00:00:32.804
Model: A trained file of numbers plus the math that uses them to turn an input into an output. For text, the input is your words and the output is a guess at the next word. Like: A compiled lookup engine: firmware image (the numbers) plus the code that reads it.

00:00:33.854 --> 00:00:35.897
Here is what travels over the wire.

00:00:36.297 --> 00:00:44.540
Your code sends a list of messages. A system message sets the rules, like you triage support tickets. A user message carries the question.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.991
The API returns the reply, plus how many tokens you used and why it stopped.

00:00:50.391 --> 00:00:58.297
API: Application programming interface: the set of calls one program offers another. Here, usually an HTTP endpoint you send JSON to. Like: The ioctl list a driver exposes: the only doors into it.

00:00:58.547 --> 00:01:17.576
Token: The unit an LLM reads and writes: a common word, part of a word, or a punctuation mark, each mapped to an integer ID. Prices and limits are counted in tokens. Like: A USB packet: the model never sees "characters", only fixed units it was built to process.

00:01:17.876 --> 00:01:21.382
Your code appends that reply to its own copy of the conversation.

00:01:21.782 --> 00:01:31.999
For the next turn, you send the entire history again, plus the new message. The API is stateless, like a server that keeps no session: it remembers nothing between calls.

00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:47.887
Stateless API: The model keeps no memory between calls. To "continue a chat" you resend the whole history each time (or let a server-side feature store it for you). Like: A UDP server: every packet must carry everything needed to answer it.

00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:52.981
That is why the bot in the story forgot the name. Someone sent only the latest message.

00:01:54.181 --> 00:02:02.552
Here is a real call with the official OpenAI Python library. The client reads your API key from an environment variable, never from code.

00:02:02.952 --> 00:02:05.170
You call responses dot create.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:10.283
The model name comes from configuration, because names change several times a year.

00:02:10.683 --> 00:02:14.979
Instructions play the system role, and input holds the conversation.

00:02:15.379 --> 00:02:17.701
Output text gives you the reply.

00:02:18.101 --> 00:02:29.641
And usage tells you the input and output tokens, which is what you pay for. Anthropic's Messages API has the same shape with different field names, and lab three wraps both behind one function.

00:02:30.841 --> 00:02:34.545
Agents need output that code can read, not just prose.

00:02:34.945 --> 00:02:53.045
Agent: An LLM in a loop that decides its own next step: it calls tools, reads the results, and keeps going until the goal is met or a limit is hit. Like: A technician with a toolbox and a ticket, not a script: they choose which tool to use next based on what they just saw.

00:02:53.345 --> 00:03:00.671
Ask for JSON that matches a schema, which is like a struct definition: field names, types, allowed values.

00:03:01.071 --> 00:03:09.117
JSON: A plain-text format for structured data: objects in {braces}, lists in [brackets], strings in quotes. The lingua franca of web APIs. Like: A struct printed as text so any language can read it back.

00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:24.692
JSON Schema: A standard JSON document that describes what valid JSON must look like: which fields, their types, which are required. Like: A C header file for data: it declares the struct so both sides agree on the layout.

00:03:24.992 --> 00:03:30.623
Use the provider's structured output feature when the model supports it. It makes malformed JSON rare.

00:03:31.023 --> 00:03:46.499
Structured output: Making the model reply in a machine-readable shape, usually JSON that matches a schema you give, instead of free prose. Like: A form with labelled boxes instead of a blank page: easy for the next program to read.

00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:54.101
Still validate every reply, for example with Pydantic, because the values can be wrong even when the format is right.

00:03:54.501 --> 00:04:09.710
Pydantic: A popular Python library: you declare a class with typed fields, and it validates data against it and can emit the matching JSON Schema. Like: A struct definition that also checks every incoming packet against itself.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:20.634
If validation fails, send the exact error back and ask again, a bounded number of times. It is a negative acknowledgement with a reason, so the sender can retransmit correctly.

00:04:21.834 --> 00:04:28.904
Question for you: Your chat app sends only the newest user message to the API. What goes wrong, and how do you fix it?

00:04:35.204 --> 00:04:49.171
Answer: The model sees no earlier turns, so it loses all context. The API keeps no memory between calls, so your code must send the conversation history, trimmed or summarised to fit, with every request.

00:04:50.271 --> 00:04:50.921
Recap.

00:04:51.321 --> 00:04:55.025
You send messages with roles: system, user and assistant.

00:04:55.425 --> 00:04:59.558
The API is stateless, so your code sends the history every time.

00:04:59.958 --> 00:05:03.197
Model names live in config and keys live in the environment.

00:05:03.597 --> 00:05:08.752
And reliable output means a schema, validation, and a bounded repair loop.
